! DIVIDE (/), the VFL integer form, opcode abc011101.  Reference
! Manual printed p.70 defines the binary operation in one paragraph:
!
!   "If a binary operation is specified, the ENTIRE ACCUMULATOR
!   CONTENTS TO THE LEFT OF THE OFFSET are divided by the addressed
!   storage field.  Accumulator positions to the right of the offset
!   are ignored.  At the completion of the operation, the quotient is
!   placed in the CLEARED ACCUMULATOR AT THE SPECIFIED OFFSET, and
!   the remainder with sign is placed in the REMAINDER REGISTER (RM).
!   Both dividend and divisor are treated as integers, and only the
!   integral part of the quotient is developed.  The length of the
!   dividend to the left of the offset cannot exceed 96 significant
!   bits, and the divisor cannot exceed 48 significant numeric bits.
!   THE SIGN OF THE QUOTIENT REPLACES THE SIGN BIT IN THE ACCUMULATOR
!   SIGN BYTE REGISTER.  Bits 60-63 of RM form a four-bit remainder
!   sign byte with bit 60, the sign bit, SET TO THE ORIGINAL
!   ACCUMULATOR SIGN.  Bits 61-63 are set to zero.  Bits 0-59 of RM
!   are replaced by the integer remainder with the LOW-ORDER
!   REMAINDER BIT IN BIT POSITION 59 of the remainder register."
!
! THE OFFSET IS NOT A CONSTANT HERE AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE FROM
! MULTIPLY, which is the sibling deck decks/vfl_multiply.strap.  `*`
! selects its accumulator operand by the offset and then deposits the
! product at a FIXED offset 20; `/` selects its dividend by the offset
! and deposits the quotient BACK AT THAT SAME OFFSET.  R23-9694
! printed p.121: "the rightmost bit of the quotient is always found at
! the offset specified in the instruction.  The bits to the right of
! the offset are zero."
!
! AND THE CE MANUAL CARRIES A RULE THE REFERENCE MANUAL DOES NOT,
! printed p.121 under Zero Divisor: "If the divisor contains over 48
! bits, but THE LOW-ORDER 48 BITS ARE ZERO, both the PF and ZD
! indicators are turned on."  So the zero test is applied to the
! TRUNCATED divisor.  A divisor of 2**48 -- which is not zero, and
! which no reading of printed p.70 alone would make a zero divisor --
! divides by zero.  T7, T8 and T9 are that sentence.
!
! THE CONSTANTS ARE MCP'S.  printed p.24 lines 19-22 are
!
!     L(BU,26), $TC, -60           -GET TIME
!     LVI, $1, XVSTOR
!     /I(BU,6), 60, -60
!     C0011(BU,6), $RM+.54         -SECONDS IN RM IN BINARY
!
! -- read the 26-bit time clock, divide by sixty, and read the
! seconds out of the LOW SIX BITS OF RM.  Sixty seconds needs exactly
! six bits and the manual puts the remainder's low-order bit at
! position 59, so bits 54-59 are exactly the seconds.  This deck reads
! its remainder the same way, with the same "$RM+.54", because that
! read is the one the operating system actually performs.
!
! WHAT EACH CHECK ESTABLISHES
!   T1    the quotient.  1000000 divided by 60 is 16666.
!   T2    the remainder, 40, read out of RM the way MCP reads it.
!   T3    the quotient at offset 0, and
!   T4    THE SAME DIVISION AT OFFSET 40, read back from offset 0 --
!         16666 shifted left forty bits.  A model that deposited at a
!         constant (multiply's offset 20, say) would put 16666 at
!         offset 20 here and T4 would read 16666 shifted by twenty.
!         T3 and T4 together are the whole "at the specified offset".
!   T5    "the bits to the right of the offset are zero", and the
!         accumulator cleared: twenty bits of ones are placed below
!         the dividend where the offset shift discards them, and must
!         be gone afterwards.
!   T6    the IMMEDIATE form, which is the form MCP uses.
!   T7    >>> THE CE MANUAL'S RULE <<<  A divisor of 2**48 has 49
!         significant bits and its low 48 are zero, so the division
!         is NOT ATTEMPTED and the accumulator still holds the
!         dividend.  A model that tested the written divisor for zero
!         would divide 1000000 by 2**48 and store 0 here.
!   T8    and PF is on for it, and
!   T9    so is ZD -- "both the PF and ZD indicators are turned on".
!   T10   a plain zero divisor leaves the accumulator alone, and
!   T11   leaves THE REMAINDER REGISTER alone too: RM still holds the
!         40 from T2, which is what "both the accumulator and the
!         remainder register remain unchanged" claims and what a
!         model that cleared RM first would lose.
!   T12   THE TWO SIGNS GO TO DIFFERENT PLACES.  A negative dividend
!         divided by a negative divisor gives a POSITIVE quotient, so
!         the accumulator sign byte must end up positive, while
!   T13   RM's four-bit sign byte must hold the ORIGINAL ACCUMULATOR
!         sign, which is negative -- bit 60 set, bits 61-63 zero, so
!         the four bits read 8.  A model that wrote the quotient's
!         sign into RM reads 0 here and passes T12.
!   T14   a divisor that STRADDLES A WORD BOUNDARY, for the same
!         reason decks/vfl_multiply.strap has one: every other divisor
!         in this file is a word-aligned full word or an immediate, so
!         the arm's two-slot prefetch read would otherwise be copied
!         code that never ran.  Exchanging the slots reads a divisor
!         of zero, which this arm declines to divide by, so the
!         accumulator would still hold the dividend.
!
! FIVE INJECTIONS, MEASURED, all five run in ONE wall-clock in
! private copies of bsv/ rather than one compile each:
!
!   the quotient deposited at a CONSTANT offset 20, as MULTIPLY does,
!     instead of at the instruction's offset -- six checks fail and
!     T1/T3/T4 separate: T1 = T3 = 0x411A00000 (the quotient twenty
!     bits above where it is read), T4 = 0x411A00000 against
!     0x411A0000000000, T6 = 0x411A00000, T12 = 0x411A0000000,
!     T14 = 0x700000.  This is the whole difference from multiply.
!   ZD tested on the divisor AS WRITTEN instead of on its low 48
!     bits: T9 = 0 and T11 = 0.  THE CASCADE IS THE POINT -- with the
!     test moved, 2**48 is no longer a zero divisor, so the division
!     runs, the remainder register is overwritten, and T11 loses the
!     40 it was holding.  T7 still passes by coincidence, because the
!     quotient of 1000000 by the safe divisor is 1000000 again.
!   RM's sign byte taking the QUOTIENT's sign instead of the
!     DIVIDEND's: T13 = 0, alone.  That one line is why T13 exists.
!   the remainder not shifted up past its four-bit sign byte:
!     T2 = 2 and T11 = 2, which is 40 shifted four bits down.
!   the two prefetch slots exchanged on the crossing-field read:
!     T14 = 78815628 -- the dividend, untouched, because the swapped
!     slots read a divisor of zero and this arm declines to divide by
!     it.  Nothing else moves.
!
! EVERY CONSTANT IS A FULL WORD because consecutive DDs pack
! contiguously -- see the sibling deck's header for the assembly that
! proved it.  The offset counts from the accumulator's LOW-ORDER end.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

! -- the quotient and the remainder, read as MCP reads them ------
START   L(BU,64), DVD, 0           ! 1000000
        /(BU,64), DSR, 0           ! by 60
        ST(BU,64), T1, 0           ! 16666
        L(BU,6), $RM+.54, 0        ! the low six bits of RM
        ST(BU,64), T2, 0           ! 40

! -- the quotient goes to the INSTRUCTION'S offset ---------------
        L(BU,64), DVD, 0
        /(BU,64), DSR, 0           ! offset 0
        ST(BU,64), T3, 0           ! 16666

        L(BU,64), DVD, 40
        /(BU,64), DSR, 40          ! offset 40
        ST(BU,64), T4, 0           ! 16666 shifted left forty

! -- and the accumulator below the offset is cleared -------------
        L(BU,64), DVD, 40
        +(BU,64), ONES, 0          ! junk below the dividend
        /(BU,64), DSR, 40
        ST(BU,20), T5+.44, 0       ! must be 0

! -- the immediate form, which is MCP's ---------------------------
        L(BU,64), DVD, 0
        /I(BU,6), 60
        ST(BU,64), T6, 0           ! 16666

! -- a divisor whose low 48 bits are zero IS a zero divisor ------
        L(BU,64), DVD, 0
        /(BU,64), BIG49, 0         ! 2**48: not zero, low 48 are
        ST(BU,64), T7, 0           ! unchanged: still 1000000
        L(BU,1), $IND+.23, 0
        ST(BU,64), T8, 0           ! PF
        L(BU,1), $IND+.24, 0
        ST(BU,64), T9, 0           ! ZD

! -- and a plain zero divisor changes neither accumulator nor RM --
        L(BU,64), DVD, 0
        /(BU,64), ZERO, 0
        ST(BU,64), T10, 0          ! still 1000000
        L(BU,6), $RM+.54, 0
        ST(BU,64), T11, 0          ! RM still holds T2's 40

! -- the quotient's sign and the remainder's sign differ ---------
        L(B,64,8), NEGDVD, 0       ! -1000000
        /(B,64,8), NEGDSR, 0       ! by -60: quotient is POSITIVE
        ST(B,64,8), T12, 0         ! (16666<<8), sign bit clear
        L(BU,4), $RM+.60, 0        ! RM's four-bit sign byte
        ST(BU,64), T13, 0          ! 8: the ORIGINAL accumulator sign

! -- a divisor that crosses a word boundary ----------------------
        L(BU,64), CDVD, 0          ! 0xABCDEF times seven, plus three
        /(BU,24), CROSS+.56, 0     ! 0xABCDEF across the seam
        ST(BU,64), T14, 0          ! 7

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this every 64-bit
! field below would straddle two words and be refused.
        SLC, (8)2100.0

DVD     DD(BU,64,8), 1000000
DSR     DD(BU,64,8), 60
ZERO    DD(BU,64,8), 0
BIG49   DD(BU,64,8), 281474976710656   ! 2**48: 49 bits, low 48 zero
ONES    DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFF
! A signed field with byte size 8 is (magnitude << 8) | (sign << 7),
! which is how decks/plus_mg.strap writes its negative constant.
NEGDVD  DD(BU,64,8), 256000128         ! -(1000000)
NEGDSR  DD(BU,64,8), 15488             ! -(60)
CDVD    DD(BU,64,8), 78815628        ! 0xABCDEF * 7 + 3
CROSS   DD(BU,64,8), (16)AB
CROSS2  DD(BU,64,8), (16)CDEF000000000000

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T4      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T5      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T6      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T7      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T8      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T9      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T10     DD(BU,64,8), 0
T11     DD(BU,64,8), 0
T12     DD(BU,64,8), 0
T13     DD(BU,64,8), 0
T14     DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
